Stress concentration, notches and local models (@mech-stress-concentration: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Cracks-stress-intensity-and-energy-release.lingua#r_60ER9GS96VCBQZ4W33NH72MKP9") { r_7JY6527H7SYK4QACR0H8QCBVA9
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Closed-Form Solutions and Introduction to Fracture Mechanics; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), chapters 5 and 9.

A hole, shoulder, groove or load introduction changes the local stress field. An elastic concentration factor K_t relates a specified local elastic stress to a specified nominal stress for a declared geometry and loading. The nominal area and stress convention must match the factor's definition.

For an ideal infinite plate with a circular hole under remote uniaxial tension, the classic elastic edge maximum is three times the remote stress. Finite width, a different loading state or a noncircular hole changes the result. A sharp mathematical re-entrant corner can generate a singular stress rather than a mesh-convergent finite maximum.

A fatigue notch factor is not automatically equal to an elastic concentration factor. Material length scales, gradient, surface, residual stress and local plasticity influence the observed fatigue response. If the elastic prediction exceeds the material's linear range, resolve the appropriate local constitutive behavior instead of interpreting the elastic peak as a measured physical stress.

**Work:** Compare a circular hole, a rounded shoulder and an ideal sharp corner. State the quantity expected to converge under mesh refinement in each case. Relate a fillet change to both nominal stiffness and local stress, then explain how manufacturing variability and surface damage enter the later fatigue argument.

